<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jade Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yudi, Heavenly Grandfather, Tiāngōng, Jade Lord, Yu Huang Dadi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jade Emperor is a god of Chinese myth and folk religion, one of the representations of the primordial god. In Taoist theology he is the assistant of Yuanshi Tianzun, one of the Three Pure Ones, and he is often identified with Śakra in Chinese Buddhist cosmology. In myth he was the head of the pantheon, the supreme sovereign proclaimed by gods, immortals and humans after he defeated an evil entity that had overcome the gods; one story makes him the father of Zhinü, the weaver girl. His birthday is said to fall on the ninth day of the first lunar month, when Taoist temples hold a ritual at which worshippers burn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> and make food offerings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jade-emperor.jpg" alt="Jade Emperor" width="330" height="599" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknow (Ming Dynasty) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:玉皇大帝畫像.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At its summit are the Three Pure Ones, personifications of the Dao, one of whom is Laozi deified; below them are the <b>Jade Emperor</b>, the gods of the stars, the mountains and the body, local and household gods and famous immortals.</blockquote><a href="/religion/daoism">Daoism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Chinese name Zijincheng, first formally recorded in 1576, links it with the North Star, the heavenly home of the <b>Jade Emperor</b>, so that the earthly emperor's residence mirrored the celestial one.</blockquote><a href="/work/forbidden-city">Forbidden City · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
